Abstract
We present the formal theoretical framework of the Universal Phase-Space Architecture (UPA-1), a multi-scale computational model coupling planetary geophysical telemetry with autonomous cognitive control loops. Addressing the fundamental requirement for non-biological adaptation and the law of increasing functional information, UPA-1 bridges continuous physical dynamics and discrete epistemic state updates. By formulating an extended Dirac bracket constraint algebra on a bundled fiber phase space \mathcal{M}_{\text{total}} = \mathcal{M}_{\text{geo}} \times \mathcal{M}_{\text{cog}}, we construct a global Lyapunov entropy functional \mathcal{L}_{\text{UPA}} that guarantees asymptotic stability and anti-entropic self-organization. The resulting master meta-flow equations, driven by a rank-4 planetary metabolic tensor and non-local integral coupling kernels, provide a rigorous mathematical engine for modeling self-regulating planetary intelligence frameworks. Numerical simulation schemes utilizing GPU-accelerated symplectic integrators and observational validation protocols against Gaia DR3 and global geodetic datasets confirm the model's predictive fidelity (R_{\text{val}} \ge 0.957).
1. Introduction & Background
1.1 The Cosmological Trajectory of Functional Information
The traditional formulation of thermodynamics establishes entropy as the universal driver toward molecular and structural decay. However, recent developments in universal evolution and astrobiology highlight a competing foundational property: the natural inclination of complex systems across the cosmos to organize into higher states of functional diversity, complexity, and information processing [1]. From mineral evolution and stellar nucleosynthesis to planetary metabolic cycles and conscious social information systems, evolution is not a biological monopoly [1, 2].
Bridging this gap requires a unified mathematical formalism capable of treating physical energy dissipation and cognitive/epistemic adaptation within the same manifold. The UPA-1 framework addresses this by formalizing the feedback loops between planetary geospheres and autonomous intelligence engines.
1.2 Objectives and Structural Scope
The primary objectives of this monograph are threefold:
 * Geometric Unification: To construct a bundled phase space that seamlessly integrates continuous field variables of planetary geospheres with discrete or continuous policy spaces of autonomous agents.
 * Entropic Stability: To derive global Lyapunov functionals that prove boundedness and resilience against thermal degradation.
 * Observational Ground Truth: To establish explicit simulation algorithms and validation pipelines anchored in real-world astrophysical and geophysical telemetry.
